      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-184524",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose we wish to construct a proof by contrapositive of the following statement:\n\n> Let $n$ be an integer. Then, $n$ is odd if $2 - 3n$ is odd.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$nis odd$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nIf $n$ is {{field-3}}, then $n =$ {{field-4}}, where $a$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nSubstituting {{field-6}} into the expression $2 - 3n$, we can show that $2 - 3n =$ {{field-7}}, where $b$ is {{field-8}}.\n\nTherefore, since $2 - 3n$ {{field-9}} a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $2 - 3n$ is {{field-10}}."
